│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ shared_nonlocal_crz                                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The current circuit uses three distinct parameters, two repeated butterfly stages, depth  │
│                          │ 30, and convergence around 60 steps. Although parity-specific mixing slightly improves    │
│                          │ the worst-group margin, it reduces parameter efficiency and increases the generalization  │
│                          │ gap.                                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The new ansatz returns to one compact multiscale stage and ties all local RY/RX rotations │
│                          │ to `global_mix`. A second parameter, `global_link`, is used only by the four distance-4   │
│                          │ CRZ gates, concentrating limited extra capacity on nonlocal correlations that are most    │
│                          │ relevant to worst-group separation. Distance-1 and distance-2 CZ links remain fixed       │
│                          │ regularizers. This reduces the circuit depth and gate count by roughly half while using   │
│                          │ only two distinct trainable parameters, targeting faster convergence and better parameter │
│                          │ efficiency without removing focused nonlocal adaptability.                                │
